{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLeiUQcY1KsR9f3ste4S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018TGn4Mqhp6SkKwwqSPW7Jk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n \"needs-human\" docs/proposal.md | head -20; sed -n 50,56p CLAUDE.md; sed -n 1,80p internal/roles/cve_docker_integration_test.go; grep -n \"ARD_GITLEAKS\\|TestGitleaksReal\\|ARD_.*_BIN\" internal/roles/*.go | head; grep -n \"diff_max\\|diff_warn\" .ard/config.yaml; grep -n \"^test\\|^lint\" Makefile"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2493,"cache_read_input_tokens":16131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2493},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96f8d4a9-7666-4e99-9ffe-07f2b6088bfc","uuid":"2502f91b-d6c2-4fdf-b101-a511ad87055c","timestamp":"2026-11-23T22:30:16.038Z","request_id":"req_011ChLeiUFgDs2YmZtVobmf6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018TGn4Mqhp6SkKwwqSPW7Jk","type":"tool_result","content":"50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result, or a scanner at filing (`route: needs-human`) | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n129:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n138:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\n156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n183:**Route.** The scanner key `route` decides where its issues go: `pending` (default for executable scanners) files with `ard:pending`, so they enter triage; `none` (default for LLM scanners) files with the scanner label only, and a human adds `ard:pending` to send one issue to triage; `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` and no `ard:pending`, so no agent triages them. LLM scanners may not use `pending`. Changing `route` affects only new issues. A human promotes one issue by removing `ard:needs-human` and/or adding `ard:pending`; after that ard never joins findings onto it.\n188:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner with those routes stops once its issues fill `max_open` until a human closes or promotes some): a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\n206:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n225:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is not scanned. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile and is not in the published `ard` image.\n237:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\n247:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human.\n249:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 2,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n251:**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\n253:Three cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\n255:Before it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n261:| lead | nothing (role file only); writes `triage-lead.md` with routing, or `needs-human` when a person must decide | every `ard:pending` issue |\n268:Each triage role owns one `##` section of `plan.md` with a fixed heading (Requirements, Design, UX, Docs, Implementation; a custom triage role names its own with `plan_section`). The first writer creates the file with `# Plan for #<issue#>`. A role replaces its own section in place on a re-run, never edits another role's section (close-out enforces it), and leaves the file untouched on `blocked` or `needs-human`.\n- Tests: table-driven, no network. Use the GitHub fake for unit tests; real-API tests go behind the `integration` build tag.\n- Errors: wrap with context (`fmt.Errorf(\"claim issue %d: %w\", n, err)`); never swallow.\n- Logging: structured (`log/slog`), one event per claim, close-out and failure.\n- Keep packages small and interfaces at the consumer.\n\n## Workflow\n\n//go:build integration\n\npackage roles\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// An older base image than scanners/cve/Dockerfile's, so a go 1.27 module is\n// \"newer than the image\" while 1.27.4 is still the newest Go release. Digest\n// from `docker buildx imagetools inspect`, 2026-11-21.\nconst oldGoImage = \"golang:1.26.11-bookworm@sha256:dc40a13b61304647ed7e5d581d246b86413dfc29d63c6d018310feb93cd98fc0\"\n\nfunc dockerOut(t *testing.T, timeout time.Duration, args ...string) (stdout, stderr string, code int) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), timeout)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, \"docker\", args...)\n\tvar so, se bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd.Stdout, cmd.Stderr = &so, &se\n\tif err := cmd.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\tee, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"docker %v: %v\", args, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tcode = ee.ExitCode()\n\t}\n\treturn so.String(), se.String(), code\n}\n\n// scanRunArgs mirrors the executor's runArgv for a read-only scanner job.\n// Keep in sync with internal/executor/local/docker.go runArgv.\nfunc scanRunArgs(image, repo, roleDir string, extraEnv ...string) []string {\n\ta := []string{\"run\", \"--rm\", \"--init\",\n\t\t\"--user\", fmt.Sprintf(\"%d:%d\", os.Getuid(), os.Getgid()),\n\t\t\"--cap-drop\", \"ALL\", \"--security-opt\", \"no-new-privileges\", \"--tmpfs\", \"/tmp\",\n\t\t\"-v\", repo + \":/work:ro\", \"-v\", roleDir + \":/role:ro\",\n\t\t\"-w\", \"/work\", \"-e\", \"HOME=/tmp\", \"-e\", \"LANG=C.UTF-8\", \"-e\", \"GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0\"}\n\tfor _, kv := range extraEnv {\n\t\ta = append(a, \"-e\", kv)\n\t}\n\treturn append(a, image, \"/role/scan\")\n}\n\nfunc buildCVEImage(t *testing.T, tag string, buildArgs ...string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\targs := append([]string{\"build\", \"-q\", \"-t\", tag}, buildArgs...)\n\targs = append(args, \"scanners/cve\")\n\tif _, se, code := dockerOut(t, 15*time.Minute, args...); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"docker build: %s\", se)\n\t}\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { dockerOut(t, time.Minute, \"image\", \"rm\", tag) })\n}\n\n// TestIntegrationDocker runs the real cve role in its real image under the\n// executor's container flags (noexec /tmp, read-only worktree) against a\n// module whose go directive is newer than the image's Go. Needs Docker, a\n// host Go >= 1.27 and network.\nfunc TestIntegrationDocker(t *testing.T) {\n\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"docker\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Skip(\"docker not on PATH\")\n\t}\n\tif _, _, code := dockerOut(t, 30*time.Second, \"info\"); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Skip(\"docker daemon not available\")\n\t}\n\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"go\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Skip(\"go not on PATH\")\n\t}\n\trepo := t.TempDir()\n\twrite := func(name, s string) {\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(repo, name), []byte(s), 0o644); err != nil {\ninternal/roles/scanner_guard_test.go:15:// output: a real scanner's findings must not trip it. With ARD_GITLEAKS_BIN\ninternal/roles/scanner_guard_test.go:19:\tif bin := os.Getenv(\"ARD_GITLEAKS_BIN\"); bin != \"\" {\n9:test:\n12:lint:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96f8d4a9-7666-4e99-9ffe-07f2b6088bfc","uuid":"e01a6da9-1e64-4387-b9f0-0fcf460e7857","timestamp":"2026-11-23T22:30:16.091Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\n63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\n81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result, or a scanner at filing (`route: needs-human`) | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n127:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n129:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n138:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\n156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n183:**Route.** The scanner key `route` decides where its issues go: `pending` (default for executable scanners) files with `ard:pending`, so they enter triage; `none` (default for LLM scanners) files with the scanner label only, and a human adds `ard:pending` to send one issue to triage; `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` and no `ard:pending`, so no agent triages them. LLM scanners may not use `pending`. Changing `route` affects only new issues. A human promotes one issue by removing `ard:needs-human` and/or adding `ard:pending`; after that ard never joins findings onto it.\n188:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner w